{"name":"Green Ribbon","hex":"#288f3d","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"green ribbon","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A ribbon worn as a badge by the Levellers during the English Civil War."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green_ribbon"}],"entries":[{"title":"Green ribbon","blocks":[{"text":"The green ribbon can have a variety of symbolic meanings."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green_ribbon","wikidata":"Q3269517"},{"title":"Ribbon","blocks":[{"text":"A ribbon is a thin band of material, typically cloth but also plastic or sometimes metal, used primarily as decorative binding and tying. Cloth ribbons are made of natural materials such as silk, cotton, and jute and of synthetic materials, such as polyester, nylon, and polypropylene. Ribbon is used for useful, ornamental, and symbolic purposes. Cultures around the world use ribbon in their hair, around the body, and as ornament on non-human animals, buildings, and packaging. Some popular fabrics used to make ribbons are satin, organza, sheer, silk, velvet, and grosgrain."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg/330px-Scrummy_ribbons_by_Lainey_Powell.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ribbon","wikidata":"Q6918773"},{"title":"Green","blocks":[{"text":"Green is the color between cyan and yellow on the visible spectrum. It has 295–350 different shades. It is evoked by light which has a dominant wavelength of roughly 495–570 nm. In subtractive color systems, used in painting and color printing, it is created by a combination of yellow and cyan; in the RGB color model, used on television and computer screens, it is one of the additive primary colors, along with red and blue, which are mixed in different combinations to create all other colors. By far the largest contributor to green in nature is chlorophyll, the chemical by which plants photosynthesize and convert sunlight into chemical energy. Many creatures have adapted to their green environments by taking on a green hue themselves as camouflage. Several minerals have a green color, including the emerald, which is colored green by its chromium content."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg/330px-Champ_de_bl%C3%A9_C%C3%B4te-d%27Or_Bourgogne_avril_2014.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Green","wikidata":"Q3133"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg/500px-Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg/1280px-Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg/500px-Green_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_%282021-08-21%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Green ribbon used as a sign against the Russian invasion of Ukraine","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGreen_ribbon_as_symbol_of_Russian_opposition_(2021-08-21).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Jade_ribbon.png/120px-Jade_ribbon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cf/Jade_ribbon.png/250px-Jade_ribbon.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://